## Triage after this incident

This record supersedes [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).
The accepted historical decision remains unchanged. Match reports by demonstrated cause,
using the tenant's locale, booking form and channel. Neither equal subjects nor intake
labels prove that reports share a cause. Different subjects can describe the same cause.
If the tenant is unknown or the cause cannot be established, investigate rather than merge.

## What #4471 contains

The export repeats report IDs across overlapping pages. Deduplicate by ID before filtering
for #4471 and grouping by cause. The table below computes the counts from the export and
tenant directory. Browser locale and intake tags are evidence of intake's guess, not the
source of truth. The table's “no ticket of its own” label describes the exported desk state.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The shared parser reads month first for day-first tenants. Separately, booking-v3 prints
MM/DD/YYYY while interpreting the submitted date in tenant order. Fixing the parser does
not itself fix the form hint. File one issue per cause, with its distinct report count.

The reports described by the desk as a one-day move stay in the parser count under the
export's tenant-based classifier. Keep that observation visible and investigate it if new
evidence appears; it does not by itself justify inventing another cause.

## What the timeline shows

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received dates describe when reports arrived. Merged dates describe desk processing.
A merge spike is not evidence that the bug began then. Reports were arriving before
being merged; the older “one issue, one fix queued” narrative obscured a separate cause.

## Current engineering disposition

Checked the live course challenge page on 28 October 2026: #4471 closed on 5 October.
The parser is **fixed**, recorded as “fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales”.
The hint is **won't fix**, because booking-v3 was retired. These are the desk's reported
outcomes; this export alone cannot verify a production rollout. Its 4 October snapshot
predates those outcomes. Do not copy its “fix queued” status as current advice.

## Apply the replacement rule

<DedupeExplorer />

Read the rendered comparisons before merging. The same tenant and subject can still
produce different causes through different channels. Unknown tenants require investigation.
The old triage page and incident narrative are historical and conflict with these live
computed views; use this replacement record for new triage and recheck status when reviewing.

